---
// src/pages/native.astro — native controls on the same page as the island.
//
// Adding a renderer does not take the native components away: `<ThemeToggle />`
// is still a real <button> with no island and no client:load, and
// `<ThemeFamilySelect />` still wires itself. Both drive the one runtime the
// island installs, so the two surfaces cannot disagree.
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ThemeToggle from "@themvora/astro/ThemeToggle.astro";
import ThemeFamilySelect from "@themvora/astro/ThemeFamilySelect.astro";
import ThemeIsland from "../components/ThemeIsland";
import themeConfig from "../../theme.config";
---

<BaseLayout>
  <main>
    <h1>Native controls + island</h1>

    <section class="surface">
      <h2>Astro, no client framework</h2>

      <ThemeFamilySelect themes={themeConfig.themes} />

      <ThemeToggle class="btn" label="Toggle light/dark" />
      <ThemeToggle class="btn" showMode ariaLabel="Toggle colour scheme" />

      <p class="muted">
        family: <span data-tv-readout="family">—</span>
        · mode: <span data-tv-readout="mode">—</span>
        · theme: <span data-tv-readout="theme">—</span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <ThemeIsland client:load />

    <section class="surface">
      <h2>One runtime, two surfaces</h2>

      <p>
        The select and the toggles above are plain Astro components — real form
        controls, no island. The panel below them is React. Both drive the
        single runtime the island installs, so the readouts, the controls and
        the island's own text cannot disagree.
      </p>

      <p>
        The overlay scrollbar on this page is the same one the island page
        mounts: it is in the layout, so it survives client-side navigation
        instead of being rebuilt per page.
      </p>

      <p>
        Scroll, then toggle from the native button. The rail re-colours without
        being torn down, and the island's text follows in the same frame.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the overlay lives in the layout</h2>

      <p>
        Mounting it per page would recreate the overlay on every navigation and
        leave a gap where neither scrollbar is drawn. In the layout it is
        mounted once, and the client router keeps the element — so a
        navigation only re-measures it.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is also the reason the layout passes <code>scrollbar</code> to the
        provider: the class and the hiding CSS are then in the server-rendered
        HTML, so the native bar is never painted in the first place. Without
        that pairing the overlay still initializes, but only after the first
        paint, and the native bar is visible until then.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p>
      <a href="/">Back to the island page</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
